From: Gemini PDA Linux bring-up
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: mediatek: mt6797: cpu-map and CPU capacities

Two omissions that together made the scheduler blind to this SoC's shape.

Without a cpu-map, arm64's MPIDR fallback reports each cluster as a separate
*package* (package_id = Aff1), and the scheduler builds three disjoint domain
trees: no domain spans more than one cluster, so nothing ever balances between
them. A task that starts on an A53 can never reach an idle A72; the two A53
clusters could not balance with each other either, and never could. Measured
on the Gemini PDA (2026-08-24): every CPU's only sched domain was MC over its
own cluster. With the map, all ten CPUs form one package of three clusters and
one domain spans the machine.

Without capacity-dmips-mhz, every CPU defaults to capacity 1024 at
sched_init_domains() time — and that moment is the only unconditional run of
asym_cpu_capacity_scan(). Capacities set later (e.g. from a driver initcall)
are read back correctly in sysfs but the asymmetry list stays empty forever:
partition_sched_domains_locked() only rescans when arch_update_cpu_topology()
reports a change, which plain hotplug never does. So SD_ASYM_CPUCAPACITY never
appears and misfit migration never runs. Declared in the DT, the scan sees a
big.LITTLE machine from the first domain build.

The values are the measured single-thread throughput of each cluster at its
ceiling clock (gbench stint): A53-LL 642 Mops/s @1222 MHz -> 608, A53-L 689
@1209 -> 617, A72 1143 @2002 -> 1024. The at-ceiling ratio is encoded directly
because the big cluster has no cpufreq policy, so the kernel's frequency-based
renormalisation (which waits for every CPU's policy) never completes and the
raw values become the final capacities.

Effect, measured: an unpinned CPU hog that starts on an A53 migrates to an
idle A72 within ~300 ms and runs 1.6x faster; before, it never moved.

---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6797.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6797.dtsi
@@ -26,6 +26,17 @@
 		#address-cells = <1>;
 		#size-cells = <0>;
 
+		/*
+		 * Measured single-thread throughput at each cluster's ceiling
+		 * (gbench stint, Gemini PDA 2026-08-24): A53-LL 642 Mops/s at
+		 * 1222 MHz, A53-L 689 at 1209, A72 1143 at 2002. Encoded as
+		 * the at-ceiling ratio because the big cluster has no cpufreq
+		 * policy, so the kernel's frequency renormalisation never runs
+		 * and these raw values become the scheduler capacities as-is.
+		 * They are what makes asym_cpu_capacity_scan() see a
+		 * big.LITTLE machine at boot — set later (e.g. from a driver),
+		 * the scan has already run symmetric and never reruns.
+		 */
 		cpu0: cpu@0 {
 			device_type = "cpu";
 			compatible = "arm,cortex-a53";
@@ -34,6 +45,7 @@
 			clocks = <&mcumixed CLK_MCU_LL_SEL>,
 				 <&topckgen CLK_TOP_ARMPLL_DIV_PLL1>;
 			clock-names = "cpu", "intermediate";
+			capacity-dmips-mhz = <608>;
 		};
 
 		cpu1: cpu@1 {
@@ -44,6 +56,7 @@
 			clocks = <&mcumixed CLK_MCU_LL_SEL>,
 				 <&topckgen CLK_TOP_ARMPLL_DIV_PLL1>;
 			clock-names = "cpu", "intermediate";
+			capacity-dmips-mhz = <608>;
 		};
 
 		cpu2: cpu@2 {
@@ -54,6 +67,7 @@
 			clocks = <&mcumixed CLK_MCU_LL_SEL>,
 				 <&topckgen CLK_TOP_ARMPLL_DIV_PLL1>;
 			clock-names = "cpu", "intermediate";
+			capacity-dmips-mhz = <608>;
 		};
 
 		cpu3: cpu@3 {
@@ -64,6 +78,7 @@
 			clocks = <&mcumixed CLK_MCU_LL_SEL>,
 				 <&topckgen CLK_TOP_ARMPLL_DIV_PLL1>;
 			clock-names = "cpu", "intermediate";
+			capacity-dmips-mhz = <608>;
 		};
 
 		cpu4: cpu@100 {
@@ -74,6 +89,7 @@
 			clocks = <&mcumixed CLK_MCU_L_SEL>,
 				 <&topckgen CLK_TOP_ARMPLL_DIV_PLL1>;
 			clock-names = "cpu", "intermediate";
+			capacity-dmips-mhz = <617>;
 		};
 
 		cpu5: cpu@101 {
@@ -84,6 +100,7 @@
 			clocks = <&mcumixed CLK_MCU_L_SEL>,
 				 <&topckgen CLK_TOP_ARMPLL_DIV_PLL1>;
 			clock-names = "cpu", "intermediate";
+			capacity-dmips-mhz = <617>;
 		};
 
 		cpu6: cpu@102 {
@@ -94,6 +111,7 @@
 			clocks = <&mcumixed CLK_MCU_L_SEL>,
 				 <&topckgen CLK_TOP_ARMPLL_DIV_PLL1>;
 			clock-names = "cpu", "intermediate";
+			capacity-dmips-mhz = <617>;
 		};
 
 		cpu7: cpu@103 {
@@ -104,6 +122,7 @@
 			clocks = <&mcumixed CLK_MCU_L_SEL>,
 				 <&topckgen CLK_TOP_ARMPLL_DIV_PLL1>;
 			clock-names = "cpu", "intermediate";
+			capacity-dmips-mhz = <617>;
 		};
 
 		cpu8: cpu@200 {
@@ -111,6 +130,7 @@
 			compatible = "arm,cortex-a72";
 			enable-method = "psci";
 			reg = <0x200>;
+			capacity-dmips-mhz = <1024>;
 		};
 
 		cpu9: cpu@201 {
@@ -118,6 +138,40 @@
 			compatible = "arm,cortex-a72";
 			enable-method = "psci";
 			reg = <0x201>;
+			capacity-dmips-mhz = <1024>;
+		};
+
+		/*
+		 * Without this map, arm64's MPIDR fallback reports each cluster
+		 * as a separate PACKAGE (package_id = Aff1), so the scheduler
+		 * builds three disjoint domain trees and never balances between
+		 * clusters at all: a task that starts on an A53 can never reach
+		 * an idle A72, and misfit migration has no domain to act in
+		 * (measured on the Gemini PDA, 2026-08-24 — each CPU's only
+		 * domain was MC over its own cluster). With the map, all ten
+		 * CPUs share one package split into three clusters, the
+		 * full-span domain carries SD_ASYM_CPUCAPACITY, and big.LITTLE
+		 * placement works.
+		 */
+		cpu-map {
+			cluster0 {
+				core0 { cpu = <&cpu0>; };
+				core1 { cpu = <&cpu1>; };
+				core2 { cpu = <&cpu2>; };
+				core3 { cpu = <&cpu3>; };
+			};
+
+			cluster1 {
+				core0 { cpu = <&cpu4>; };
+				core1 { cpu = <&cpu5>; };
+				core2 { cpu = <&cpu6>; };
+				core3 { cpu = <&cpu7>; };
+			};
+
+			cluster2 {
+				core0 { cpu = <&cpu8>; };
+				core1 { cpu = <&cpu9>; };
+			};
 		};
 	};
 
